\title{Compact Space}
\taxon{Definition}
\p{
    An \strong{open cover} of a topological space #{X} is a collection
    #{\mathcal{U}} of open subsets such that
    ##{
        X=\bigcup_{U\in\mathcal{U}}U.
    }
    A \strong{finite subcover} is a finite subcollection
    #{\mathcal{V}\subseteq\mathcal{U}} such that
    ##{
        X=\bigcup_{V\in\mathcal{V}}V.
    }
    The finite subcollection may be empty.
}
\p{
    The space #{X} is \strong{compact} if every open cover of #{X} has a
    finite subcover. A subset #{K\subseteq X} is compact when it is compact
    with its [subspace topology](def-000D).
}
\p{
    Following Munkres, compactness here does not include the
    [Hausdorff](def-005S) condition. Some texts call this property
    \em{quasi-compactness} and reserve \em{compact} for Hausdorff spaces with
    the finite-subcover property.
}
